# gdlint configuration. See AGENTS §8 (verification loop) and §10a (a docstring is not evidence).
#
# THIS FILE IS A DIFF AGAINST gdtoolkit's DEFAULTS, not a replacement for them.
# gdlint fills every entry absent here from its own defaults on load
# (`_update_config_with_missing_entries_inplace`), so omitting a rule leaves it ON at
# its default value. Adding a key here is therefore a deliberate override and nothing
# else is weakened by the omission.

# --- max-file-lines: 2000, up from gdtoolkit's default 1000 -------------------------
#
# Carried over from MHZ_Materials' `.gdlintrc` with the same reasoning, not re-derived from measurement:
# no code exists in this repo yet, so there is no line-count evidence of our own to cite. Revisit this
# number once real files exist here, the way Materials did, rather than trusting it forever on the
# strength of a sibling project.
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS LIKELY WRONG FOR THIS REPO ANYWAY. 1000 is a generic gdtoolkit default for all
# GDScript everywhere — not a Godot limit, not a performance limit, and not a decision anyone here made.
# `max-file-lines` counts EVERY line, comments included, and AGENTS §10a makes maintained comments an
# evidence requirement: `core/` classes that carry non-obvious CONTRACT behaviour (the attach model, the
# SDF op order) are expected to document *why*, not just *what*, the same way `docs/SHIP_BUILDER_SPEC.md`
# does. The default 1000-line cap taxes exactly the practice the project rules mandate.
#
# THE CAP IS A SMOKE ALARM, NOT AN ORDER TO SPLIT. When it fires, the question is whether a real seam
# exists — not where line 2000 happens to fall. A good extraction is a self-contained, statically
# testable unit with no reference back to the file it came from (see `ResolvedShape` vs. `ShapeGen` in
# `docs/API_CONTRACT.md` — already split along exactly that line). Splitting a file at an arbitrary line
# to satisfy a number yields two halves that reach into each other, which is strictly worse than one long
# file. If the cap fires and there is no seam, revisit this number rather than inventing one.
#
# --- RAISED TO 2400 on 2026-09-29, with evidence, which is what this note asked for ------------
#
# The paragraph above says the 2000 was carried over from a sibling project, "not re-derived from
# measurement", and asks for it to be revisited "once real files exist here". They do now, and the
# revisit is overdue: `ship_view3d.gd` hit the cap five times in two days.
#
# FOUR REAL EXTRACTIONS WERE TAKEN FROM IT FIRST, which is the order this note prescribes:
#   ShipFlyMode    entering/leaving fly, the void, the torch            (a genuine seam)
#   ShipViewGrid   floor grid, beacon, bbox brackets, mass cross        (pure geometry)
#   ShipViewEnv    background, ambient, tonemap, clay AO                (pure, static)
# After all of them the file was STILL at the cap, and the fifth firing was paid for by deleting
# sentences out of docstrings written the same day - the failure mode this file already names: the
# cap "taxes exactly the practice the project rules mandate" (AGENTS 10a makes maintained, reasoned
# comments an evidence requirement, not a nicety).
#
# What is left in that file is one cohesive job - input routing, picking, the gizmo drag, the ghost
# - and splitting it again would produce halves that reach into each other, which this note rightly
# calls "strictly worse than one long file".
#
# 2400 leaves real headroom rather than fitting today's number: ship_view3d.gd is 2004 and
# ship_builder.gd 1947, so this is ~20% of room, not a ceiling to ride. A file that reaches 2400 is
# worth asking about again.
max-file-lines: 2400

# --- max-public-methods: 30, up from gdtoolkit's default 20 --------------------------
#
# Raised deliberately, with a named exception rather than a blanket relaxation, because it
# was actively distorting design. `harness/builder/ship_builder.gd` is the application root:
# it owns the ShipDoc, ShipData, ShipConfig, ShipHistory, ShipTheme and the 3D view, and it is
# the ONLY surface the panels in `harness/panels/` are allowed to talk to (they must not reach
# into each other, AGENTS section 3). A facade for five subsystems plus an edit protocol
# (begin_edit / commit_edit) does not fit in 20, and the agent that wrote it reported having to
# treat the cap as a hard budget where "adding one requires removing one".
#
# That is the failure mode this override exists to prevent. A lint cap that forces a public
# accessor to be deleted does not reduce coupling; it pushes callers toward reaching around the
# facade into `get_doc()` and mutating the document directly, which is strictly worse and is
# exactly what the edit protocol exists to stop.
#
# THIS IS NOT A LICENCE FOR WIDE CLASSES ELSEWHERE. Every other class in this repo should sit far
# under 20 — `core/` classes are static-only or small records by construction (AGENTS section 3),
# and a `core/` class approaching this number is a design smell, not a lint problem. If a SECOND
# class ever needs this headroom, that is the signal to revisit the facade, not the number.
max-public-methods: 30
